(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-gi-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"GI Physiology — Quiz 1 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Peristalsis differs from segmentation in that peristalsis...","choices":["propels contents forward along the tract, while segmentation mainly mixes them","occurs only in the esophagus and nowhere else in the gut during this particular process","only mixes contents, while segmentation moves them forward","is a voluntary movement, while segmentation is involuntary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peristalsis is a progressive wave of contraction behind the bolus that moves contents onward, whereas segmentation is alternating localized contractions that chop and mix contents with digestive juices without net forward movement. — GI Physiology, slides 40-46"},{"q":"During swallowing, the pharyngeal phase is an involuntary reflex that protects the airway by...","choices":["releasing bile to help move the bolus along under most conditions in the body","relaxing the diaphragm to pull the bolus downward","opening the airway wide so the bolus can pass through it","elevating the larynx and covering it with the epiglottis as the bolus passes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once swallowing is initiated, the pharyngeal phase is a rapid involuntary reflex: the soft palate closes the nasopharynx, breathing is briefly inhibited, and the larynx rises so the epiglottis covers the airway, directing the bolus into the esophagus and preventing aspiration. — GI Physiology, slides 40-45"},{"q":"Between meals, the migrating motor complex serves to...","choices":["absorb the bulk of dietary nutrients into the blood","sweep residual contents and bacteria along the gut during fasting","trigger the release of bile in response to fat during this process","mix a large meal thoroughly with digestive enzymes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The migrating motor complex is a cyclic pattern of contractions during fasting (associated with motilin) that sweeps leftover food particles, secretions, and bacteria toward the colon — a 'housekeeping' function that keeps the small intestine clear between meals. — GI Physiology, slides 40-46"},{"q":"The basic rhythm of gastrointestinal smooth-muscle contractions is set by...","choices":["hormones released only during the intestinal phase","conscious signals sent from the cerebral cortex","slow waves of electrical activity generated by pacemaker cells in the gut wall","the rate at which the stomach secretes acid under most conditions throughout the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pacemaker cells (the interstitial cells of Cajal) generate rhythmic slow waves of membrane potential that spread through the smooth muscle; these waves set the maximum frequency of contractions, though a contraction occurs only when a slow wave reaches threshold and fires action potentials. — GI Physiology, slides 24-33, 40-46"},{"q":"During swallowing, the lower esophageal sphincter must relax to...","choices":["allow the bolus to pass from the esophagus into the stomach","release bile into the esophagus to begin digestion","push stomach acid up into the mouth for buffering","prevent any food from entering the stomach at all during this process"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As a peristaltic wave carries the bolus down the esophagus, the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes to let it enter the stomach, then closes again to help prevent reflux of acidic gastric contents back into the esophagus. — GI Physiology, slides 40-45"},{"q":"Which hormone-action pairing is correct for the digestive response to a meal?","choices":["CCK — is the principal stimulus for gastric acid from parietal cells","Secretin — stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate to neutralize duodenal acid","Gastrin — relaxes the gallbladder and constricts the sphincter of Oddi","Motilin — is released after meals to speed nutrient absorption"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: secretin, released by duodenal acid, stimulates pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate, acting as 'nature's antacid'. — GI Physiology, slides 14-16"},{"q":"Gastrin promotes digestion primarily by...","choices":["inhibiting all secretion during the intestinal phase","contracting the gallbladder to release stored bile","neutralizing stomach acid to protect the duodenum","stimulating gastric acid secretion and gastric motility"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastrin, released by antral G-cells in response to protein and stomach distension, stimulates parietal-cell acid secretion (largely via histamine release) and increases gastric motility, preparing the stomach to process a meal. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15, 96"},{"q":"Cholecystokinin (CCK), released when fat and protein reach the small intestine, promotes fat digestion by...","choices":["contracting the gallbladder and stimulating pancreatic enzyme secretion","converting the fat directly into glucose","increasing gastric acid output to break down the fat","stimulating the stomach to empty faster during this process in the tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. CCK causes the gallbladder to contract and release stored bile while stimulating the pancreas to secrete digestive enzymes; together these deliver bile salts and lipase to the duodenum, and CCK also slows gastric emptying to match the intestine's capacity. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15, 130-133"},{"q":"Vagal (parasympathetic) stimulation of the stomach generally...","choices":["increases both gastric motility and acid secretion","decreases motility while raising acid secretion","shuts down all secretion during a meal","acts only on the pancreas, not the stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parasympathetic (vagal) input, acting largely through acetylcholine, stimulates gastric smooth muscle contraction and enhances secretion, including acid from parietal cells and gastrin release; this underlies the cephalic phase of gastric secretion. — GI Physiology, slides 88-90"},{"q":"How does gastrin most powerfully stimulate gastric acid secretion?","choices":["By directly neutralizing somatostatin's inhibition of the chief cells","By stimulating duodenal S-cells to release secretin into the circulating bloodstream","By converting pepsinogen into active pepsin within the acidic gastric lumen itself","Largely by driving ECL cells to release histamine, which acts on parietal cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: much of gastrin's potent acid effect is indirect, through histamine released from ECL cells acting on parietal cells. — GI Physiology, slides 88-91"},{"q":"The cephalic phase of gastric secretion is triggered by...","choices":["fat and protein reaching the small intestine","the sight, smell, or thought of food acting through the vagus nerve","stretch of the stomach wall as food enters","acidic chyme arriving in the duodenum after the stomach has emptied its load"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cephalic phase begins before food reaches the stomach: sensory and cognitive cues (sight, smell, taste, thought of food) drive vagal output that stimulates gastric secretion in anticipation of the meal, accounting for roughly a third of the acid response. — GI Physiology, slides 92-95"},{"q":"Secreting pancreatic proteases as inactive zymogens is important because it...","choices":["makes the enzymes work better in the acidic stomach","lets the pancreas store the enzymes for years at a time","allows the enzymes to digest carbohydrates instead of protein","prevents the enzymes from digesting the pancreas that makes them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If active proteases were produced within the pancreas, they would digest the organ's own tissue; secreting them as inactive zymogens (activated only in the intestine) protects the pancreas from self-digestion, a safeguard whose failure underlies pancreatitis. — GI Physiology, slides 101-105"},{"q":"Pancreatic amylase continues carbohydrate digestion by...","choices":["converting absorbed glucose directly into stored glycogen in the gut wall","splitting proteins into individual amino acids","emulsifying dietary fat into small droplets","breaking starch down into disaccharides within the small intestine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pancreatic amylase, secreted into the duodenum, hydrolyzes starch and glycogen into disaccharides and short oligosaccharides; brush-border enzymes then complete the job by splitting these into absorbable monosaccharides. It is the main luminal enzyme for carbohydrate digestion. — GI Physiology, slides 101, 123-124"},{"q":"Disaccharides such as lactose, sucrose, and maltose are digested by...","choices":["gastric acid in the stomach during this particular process in the tissues","brush-border enzymes on the intestinal surface just before absorption","salivary amylase acting in the mouth","bile salts secreted from the gallbladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enzymes anchored on the microvilli of the intestinal brush border — lactase, sucrase, and maltase — split disaccharides into monosaccharides right at the absorptive surface, so the resulting glucose, fructose, and galactose can be taken up immediately. — GI Physiology, slides 123-127"},{"q":"Final digestion of disaccharides such as lactose occurs...","choices":["at the brush border, where membrane-bound enzymes split them into monosaccharides","within the pancreatic duct before the sugars reach the intestine","in the stomach, where gastric acid hydrolyzes them completely into free glucose units","in the mouth, where salivary amylase completes their breakdown"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Disaccharidases such as lactase, sucrase, and maltase are fixed on the intestinal brush border; they perform the final step of carbohydrate digestion, splitting disaccharides into absorbable monosaccharides right at the absorptive surface. — GI Physiology, slides 123-125"},{"q":"The stomach lining is protected from its own acid and pepsin mainly by...","choices":["a layer of mucus and bicarbonate secreted over the epithelium","the complete absence of acid in contact with the wall","rapid peristalsis that keeps acid away from the lining","a continuous flow of bile that neutralizes the acid in the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Surface mucous cells secrete a thick, bicarbonate-rich mucus layer that coats the gastric epithelium; this barrier neutralizes acid at the cell surface and shields the lining from pepsin, so the stomach can hold strong acid without digesting itself. — GI Physiology, slides 82-90"},{"q":"Salivary amylase begins the digestion of...","choices":["fat, by emulsifying it into small droplets","nucleic acids, releasing their component nucleotides","starch, though its action is soon halted by stomach acid","protein, which it splits into individual amino acids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Salivary amylase (ptyalin) starts breaking down starch in the mouth; when the bolus reaches the acidic stomach, the low pH inactivates the enzyme, and starch digestion resumes in the small intestine with pancreatic amylase. — GI Physiology, slides 76-79, 123"},{"q":"Protein digestion is completed at the intestinal brush border, where...","choices":["bile salts emulsify the peptides into droplets in most cases","stomach acid dissolves the peptides into amino acids","peptidases split small peptides into absorbable amino acids","salivary amylase finishes breaking down the protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After stomach and pancreatic proteases break protein into small peptides, brush-border peptidases (and enzymes inside the enterocyte) complete digestion by splitting these into free amino acids and di- and tripeptides that can be absorbed. — GI Physiology, slides 123-129"},{"q":"Between meals, the gallbladder...","choices":["stores and concentrates bile by absorbing water and electrolytes","actively secretes new bile salts into the duodenum","empties continuously to keep the duodenum supplied in the body under most conditions","manufactures new bile from cholesterol entirely on its own"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. During fasting the sphincter of Oddi is closed and bile is diverted into the gallbladder, whose mucosa absorbs water and electrolytes to concentrate the bile several-fold; it is then released when a fatty meal triggers CCK. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 17"},{"q":"Bile salts are used efficiently because most are...","choices":["broken down for energy once fat absorption is complete","reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver for reuse (enterohepatic circulation)","excreted entirely in the feces after a single use under most conditions throughout the body","converted into pancreatic enzymes in the duodenum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After aiding fat absorption, the great majority of bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and carried back to the liver in the portal blood to be secreted again; this enterohepatic circulation recycles the limited bile-salt pool many times per meal. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 13-17"},{"q":"Bile contains both bile salts and bile pigments; the bile pigments (mainly bilirubin) are...","choices":["hormones that stimulate pancreatic secretion during this process","the active agents that emulsify fat in the intestine","waste products of hemoglobin breakdown excreted in the bile","enzymes that digest carbohydrates in the duodenum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bile pigments such as bilirubin are waste from the breakdown of heme (from old red cells); the liver conjugates and excretes them in bile. In contrast, bile salts are the functional emulsifiers derived from cholesterol. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 13-17"},{"q":"Glucose and fructose are absorbed by the enterocyte through different routes because...","choices":["glucose diffuses freely through the membrane while fructose requires ATP directly","glucose requires bile-salt micelles while fructose crosses as a free monosaccharide","glucose uses Na+-coupled secondary active transport; fructose uses facilitated diffusion","glucose enters by endocytosis while fructose is co-transported inward with sodium ions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: glucose (and galactose) enter by Na+-dependent SGLT1, whereas fructose enters by facilitated diffusion via GLUT5. — GI Physiology, slide 126"},{"q":"By the time colonic contents approach the rectum, the large intestine has primarily...","choices":["absorbed most of the remaining water and electrolytes to form solid feces","absorbed the bulk of dietary glucose and amino acids","added large volumes of bile to emulsify the residual dietary fat still present","secreted digestive enzymes to complete nutrient breakdown"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The main function of the colon is to reabsorb water and electrolytes from the residual chyme, progressively concentrating it into formed feces before it reaches the rectum to trigger the defecation reflex. — GI Physiology, slides 59-66"},{"q":"Rectal distension triggers defecation through which combined mechanism?","choices":["A sympathetic reflex that contracts the rectum and relaxes both sphincters","A purely voluntary process controlled entirely by the external sphincter","A hormonal signal from the colon that stimulates mass movements only","An intrinsic ENS reflex reinforced by a sacral parasympathetic cord reflex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: an intrinsic myenteric reflex is strengthened by a sacral (parasympathetic) spinal cord reflex, with voluntary external sphincter control. — GI Physiology, slides 65-67"},{"q":"Defecation can be voluntarily postponed because...","choices":["the rectum can absorb the feces if defecation is delayed","the external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle under conscious control","individual mass movements can be consciously stopped anywhere in the colon","the internal anal sphincter is under full voluntary command"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Although rectal filling relaxes the internal anal sphincter reflexly, the external anal sphincter is striated (skeletal) muscle innervated by the somatic pudendal nerves and under voluntary control, so a person can consciously delay defecation. — GI Physiology, slides 61-68"},{"q":"Because portal blood passes through the liver before reaching the body, the liver can...","choices":["return undigested food directly to the stomach","store the absorbed material permanently as bile","metabolize and detoxify many absorbed substances before they circulate","add dissolved oxygen to the blood returning from the gut before it circulates"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nutrients and other absorbed substances from the gut drain first through the portal vein into the liver, allowing it to process nutrients, store or release glucose, and detoxify drugs and toxins (first-pass metabolism) before the blood reaches the systemic circulation. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 3, 8"},{"q":"Kupffer cells within the liver contribute to defense by...","choices":["producing bile pigments for excretion into the intestine","phagocytizing bacteria and debris carried in from the gut via portal blood","secreting insulin to regulate the blood glucose level","storing iron recovered from the diet for later use under most normal conditions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Kupffer cells are resident macrophages lining the liver sinusoids; because portal blood from the intestine passes through the liver, they are positioned to engulf bacteria and debris before that blood enters the general circulation. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 6-8"},{"q":"Which statement about protein absorption is correct?","choices":["Protein absorption occurs mainly in the stomach alongside pepsin action","Peptides are absorbed intact into blood without any further breakdown","Only free amino acids can be absorbed, since peptides are never taken up at all","Di- and tripeptides are absorbed by their own carrier, then split inside the cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In addition to amino-acid transporters, the enterocyte has a distinct carrier for di- and tripeptides (which is actually faster); once inside, cytoplasmic peptidases hydrolyze these small peptides to free amino acids before they enter the blood. — GI Physiology, slides 127-129"},{"q":"Much of the absorption of nutrients like glucose and amino acids is coupled to...","choices":["the digestion of water into hydrogen and oxygen","the breakdown of the nutrients by stomach acid in most cases","the transport of sodium, whose gradient powers their uptake","the emulsifying action of bile salts on the nutrients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucose and amino acids are absorbed by sodium-coupled cotransporters: the inward sodium gradient (maintained by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase) drives these nutrients into the enterocyte against their own gradients, an example of secondary active transport. — GI Physiology, slides 126-129"},{"q":"Absorbed dietary fat reaches the systemic circulation by...","choices":["being stored within the liver for a time before any of it is finally released","traveling as chylomicrons through lymph and entering blood at the thoracic duct","binding tightly to hemoglobin so as to be carried for transport inside the red blood cells","diffusing straight into the portal vein like glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reassembled triglycerides are packaged into chylomicrons, which are too large to enter blood capillaries; they enter the lacteals of the villi, travel through the lymphatic system, and join the bloodstream where the thoracic duct empties into the venous system. — GI Physiology, slides 135-138"}]};
